The song became the trio's second number one hit on the [[Hot Country Songs]] chart dated November 28, 2009,<ref name=countrystart/> where it stayed for five weeks,<ref name=countryend/> making it the first song to hold the number one position for more than four weeks since [[Taylor Swift]]'s "[[Our Song (Taylor Swift song)|Our Song]]" spent six weeks at number one between December 2007 and January 2008.<ref name="oursong">{{cite magazine|url=http://www.billboard.com/charts/2008-01-26/country-songs|title=Country top 10 for chart week of January 26, 2008|magazine=Billboard.com|access-date=September 26, 2010}}</ref> No other song would spend more than four weeks at number one until January 2016, when [[Thomas Rhett]]'s "[[Die a Happy Man]]" spent six weeks at the top.{{citation needed|date=January 2025}}

In the [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] chart, the song peaked at number two and stayed there for two weeks, making it their highest-charting single on that chart and their first Top 5. The song also peaked at No. 2 on the [[Canadian Hot 100]], making it their highest-charting and first Top 5 single there as well. "Need You Now" is only the second country song to reach number one on the ''Billboard'' [[Adult Top 40]] chart, the first being [[Faith Hill]]'s "[[Breathe (Faith Hill song)|Breathe]]", which reached that same position in 2000.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=http://www.billboard.com/charts/2000-04-22/adult-pop-songs|title=Adult Top 40 top 10 for chart week of April 22, 2000|magazine=Billboard.com|access-date=September 26, 2010}}</ref> For the ''Billboard'' tracking period of March 29 – April 4, 2010, "Need You Now" broke the record held by [[Hoobastank]]'s hit "[[The Reason (Hoobastank song)|The Reason]]" for most spins in one week on the Adult Top 40 chart.<ref name="breathe">{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/chart-beat-wednesday-zac-brown-band-lady-antebellum-yoko-ono/|title=Billboard chart beat|magazine=Billboard.com|access-date=September 26, 2010}}</ref> The song debuted at No. 28 on the [[UK Singles Chart]] in its first week of release. It was covered in a live performance for [[Queen Elizabeth II]]'s [[Diamond Jubilee Concert]] in June 2012 by English singers [[Gary Barlow]] and [[Cheryl Cole]] which saw the song re-enter the UK Charts.<ref name="adult40record">{{cite web|url=https://www.officialcharts.com/search/singles/This%20New%20Day |title="Need You Now" UK Singles Chart |publisher=The Official Charts Company |access-date=September 26, 2010 }}</ref> Over a month later, it climbed to No. 15, its highest UK chart position and after 53 weeks in the chart.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.musicweek.com/story.asp?sectioncode=1&storycode=1050399&c=1 |title=Official Charts Analysis: Newton Faulkner needs just 16k sales to hit No.1 |access-date=July 16, 2012 |archive-date=November 2, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131102231940/http://www.musicweek.com/story.asp?sectioncode=1&storycode=1050399&c=1 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

On December 9, 2010, the song was named the No. 2 Hot 100 song of the year by ''Billboard'', behind "[[Tik Tok (song)|Tik Tok]]" by [[Kesha]], marking the highest position a country song has ever reached since [[Faith Hill]]'s "[[Breathe (Faith Hill song)|Breathe]]" was ranked No. 1 in the year-end chart of the year 2000.<ref name=hot100abc>{{cite magazine|url=http://www.billboard.com/charts/year-end/2010/hot-100-songs|title=Year-end Hot 100 songs|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|publisher=[[Nielsen Business Media, Inc]]|access-date=December 10, 2010}}</ref>

In April 2011, "Need You Now" replaced [[Taylor Swift]]'s "[[Love Story (Taylor Swift song)|Love Story]]" as the best-selling country song of all time in the U.S.<ref>{{cite web| url=http://new.music.yahoo.com/blogs/chart_watch/74349/week-ending-april-3-2011-songs-born-hot/| title=Week Ending April 3, 2011. Songs: Born Hot| work=Yahoo! Music| last=Grein| first=Paul| date=April 6, 2011| access-date=March 15, 2013}}</ref> It is also the second country song to sell over 5 million digital copies (after Swift's "Love Story").<ref>{{cite web|url=http://music.yahoo.com/blogs/chart-watch/week-ending-april-17-2011-songs-youth-will-be-served.html|title=Week Ending April 17, 2011. Songs: Youth Will Be Served|publisher=[[Yahoo! Music]]|date=April 20, 2011|access-date=April 21, 2011|last=Grein|first=Paul|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140215092440/http://music.yahoo.com/blogs/chart-watch/week-ending-april-17-2011-songs-youth-will-be-served.html|archive-date=February 15, 2014}}</ref> It has sold over 6 million copies by January 2013, the first country song to do so.<ref name="yahoo_jan2013"/> As of June 2016, it had sold 6.7 million units in the U.S.<ref name="sales2016">{{cite news |url=http://roughstock.com/news/2016/06/40523-top-30-digital-country-singles-report-june-28-2016 |title=Top 30 Digital Country Singles Report: June 28, 2016 |first=Matt |last=Bjorke |date=June 28, 2016 |work=Roughstock }}{{Dead link|date=August 2025 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

Due to the band winning four Grammys, "Need You Now" debuted at No. 32 on the [[German Singles Chart]] in February 2011, 10 months after the digital release of the single. Before that, the song did not sell enough to enter the chart.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.gfk-entertainment.com/news/roxette-gelingt-grandioses-comeback.html |title=Roxette gelingt grandioses Comeback |date=February 22, 2011 |access-date=March 15, 2013 |work=[[Media Control Charts]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150622050341/http://www.gfk-entertainment.com/news/roxette-gelingt-grandioses-comeback.html |archive-date=June 22, 2015 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.amazon.de/dp/B003BBXYIK|title=Need You Now: Lady Antebellum: Amazon.de: MP3-Downloads|work=[[amazon.com]]|access-date=March 15, 2013}}</ref>

